\section{Delimited Continuations}
\label{sec:delimited-continuations}

The continuations discussed so far are \emph{undelimited}: they represent the entire rest of the computation, all the way to the final answer. \emph{Delimited continuations} (also called \emph{partial} or \emph{segmented} continuations) capture only a \emph{portion} of the computation---from the current point up to a dynamically established delimiter.

\subsection{Motivation for Delimitation}
\label{sec:delimit-motivation}

Undelimited continuations are too powerful for many applications. When a captured continuation is invoked, it \emph{abandons} the current computation entirely. This makes it impossible to express computations that need to \emph{resume} after a captured continuation is used---a pattern needed for generators, coroutines, and backtracking.

\begin{example}[The Generator Problem]
\label{ex:generator-problem}
A generator should produce values one at a time, resuming from where it left off. With undelimited continuations, invoking the captured continuation replaces the entire computation, so the caller's context is lost. Delimited continuations solve this by capturing only the segment of the computation between the generator and its immediate caller.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Control Operators $\shift$ and $\reset$}
\label{sec:shift-reset}

Danvy and Filinski (1990) introduced the operators $\shift$ and $\reset$ for delimited control:

\begin{definition}[Delimited Control Operators]
\label{def:shift-reset}
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\reset\, e$: establishes a delimiter. Evaluates $e$; if $e$ produces a value $v$ (without invoking $\shift$), the result is $v$.
  \item $\shift\, k.\, e$: captures the delimited continuation $k$ (the computation from the $\shift$ to the nearest enclosing $\reset$) and evaluates $e$. The body $e$ has access to $k$ as a first-class function.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{semrules}{Delimited Continuation Semantics}
  \semE \sembrack{\reset\, e}\, \rho\, \kappa &= \semE \sembrack{e}\, \rho\, (\lambda v.\, \kappa\, v) \\
  \semE \sembrack{\shift\, k.\, e}\, \rho\, \kappa &= \semE \sembrack{e}\, \rho\, (\lambda v.\, \kappa\, v)\; \text{with } k = \kappa
\end{semrules}

\begin{example}[Generator with Delimited Continuations]
\label{ex:generator-delimited}
\begin{verbatim}
  let gen = reset (
    shift k. (k 1; k 2; k 3; 0)
  )
  (* gen evaluates to 1, then 2, then 3, then 0 *)
\end{verbatim}
Each call to $k$ captures the continuation up to the $\reset$, yields a value, and suspends. The caller can resume the generator to obtain the next value.
\end{example}

\subsection{Semantic Framework for Delimited Continuations}
\label{sec:delimited-semantics}

The semantics of delimited continuations requires a \emph{stack} of continuations rather than a single continuation. Each $\reset$ pushes a new continuation frame; each $\shift$ pops frames up to the nearest $\reset$.

\begin{definition}[Continuation Stack]
\label{def:cont-stack}
A \textbf{continuation stack} $K$ is a list of continuation frames $[\kappa_1, \kappa_2, \ldots, \kappa_n]$, where $\kappa_1$ is the innermost (most recently pushed) frame. The operation $\texttt{pop}(K)$ returns the top frame and the remaining stack.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Expressiveness of Delimited Continuations]
\label{thm:delimited-expressiveness}
Delimited continuations can express all the control operators that undelimited continuations can, plus additional patterns including:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Generators and iterators
  \item Coroutines with symmetric transfer
  \item Backtracking search (logic programming)
  \item Exception handling with resumption
  \item Cooperative multitasking
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\section{Continuations as Intermediate Representation}
\label{sec:continuations-as-ir}

\subsection{Why CPS as an IR?}
\label{sec:cps-as-ir-why}

Many compilers use CPS (or a CPS-like form) as their intermediate representation. The key advantages are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Explicit control flow}: All jumps, branches, and returns are represented as tail calls to continuations. There is no implicit control flow.
  \item \textbf{Uniform representation}: Functions, continuations, join points, and exception handlers are all represented uniformly as $\lambda$-abstractions.
  \item \textbf{Easy optimization}: Many optimizations become simple transformations on CPS terms.
  \item \textbf{Closure conversion}: After CPS, converting to a first-order representation (closure conversion) is straightforward.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{CPS Optimizations}
\label{sec:cps-optimizations}

Standard compiler optimizations take elegant forms in CPS:

\begin{definition}[CPS Optimization: Dead Code Elimination]
\label{def:cps-dce}
If a continuation variable $k$ is never invoked in the body of a function $\lambda k.\, \lambda x.\, e$, then the entire body is dead code and can be eliminated.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[CPS Optimization: Known-Call Optimization]
\label{def:cps-known-call}
If a function call $f\, v\, k$ is made where $f$ is bound to a known $\lambda$-abstraction $\lambda x.\, \lambda k'.\, e$, the call can be inlined: substitute $v$ for $x$ and $k$ for $k'$ in $e$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[CPS Optimization: Continuation Passing]
\label{def:cps-cont-pass}
If a function $f = \lambda x.\, \lambda k.\, g\, x\, k$ simply passes its continuation to another function $g$, then $f$ is a \emph{join point} and can be optimized by eliminating the closure allocation.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The A-Normal Form}
\label{sec:anf}

A close relative of CPS is \emph{A-normal form} (ANF), introduced by Sabry and Felleisen (1992). In ANF, every intermediate computation is bound to a variable:

\begin{definition}[A-Normal Form]
\label{def:anf}
A term is in \textbf{A-normal form} if every function call is applied to \emph{atomic} arguments (variables or constants), and every intermediate result is bound by a \texttt{let}:
\[
\texttt{let } x_1 = f(v_1, v_2) \texttt{ in let } x_2 = g(x_1, v_3) \texttt{ in } h(x_2)
\]
\end{definition}

ANF is equivalent to CPS in expressiveness but is often more convenient for certain optimizations (e.g., common subexpression elimination, constant propagation).

\begin{theorem}[ANF--CPS Equivalence]
\label{thm:anf-cps-equiv}
Every CPS term can be converted to ANF and vice versa, with at most a linear increase in size. The two representations are inter-translatable.
\end{theorem}

\section{Continuations and Effect Systems}
\label{sec:continuations-effects}

\subsection{Algebraic Effects and Handlers}
\label{sec:algebraic-effects}

Plotkin and Pretnar (2009, 2013) introduced \emph{algebraic effects and handlers}, a framework where computational effects (state, exceptions, I/O, nondeterminism) are treated as algebraic operations, and handlers interpret these operations.

\begin{definition}[Algebraic Effect]
\label{def:algebraic-effect}
An \textbf{algebraic effect} is a set of \emph{operations}, each with a type signature. For example, the state effect has operations $\texttt{get} : \unit \to \Store$ and $\texttt{put} : \Store \to \unit$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Effect Handler]
\label{def:effect-handler}
An \textbf{effect handler} interprets the operations of an effect. It provides a clause for each operation, specifying how to handle it. Each clause receives the operation's argument and a \emph{delimited continuation} representing the rest of the computation.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[State Handler]
\label{ex:state-handler}
\begin{verbatim}
  handler {
    get() -> resume(s) { s }     (* return current state *)
    put(s') -> resume(s) { resume(s') }  (* update state *)
    return v -> v                 (* pure value *)
  }
\end{verbatim}
The handler threads the state through the computation, passing the current state to \texttt{resume} and updating it on \texttt{put}.
\end{example}

\subsection{Continuations in Effect Handlers}
\label{sec:effects-continuations}

The implementation of effect handlers relies fundamentally on delimited continuations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item When an effect operation is performed, the handler captures the delimited continuation up to the handler's scope.
  \item The handler's clause for that operation is invoked with the operation's argument and the captured continuation.
  \item The clause can choose to \emph{resume} the continuation (possibly multiple times, or not at all).
\end{itemize}

This gives effect handlers remarkable expressiveness:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Exceptions}: Resume zero times (discard the continuation).
  \item \textbf{Nondeterminism}: Resume multiple times (explore all branches).
  \item \textbf{Backtracking}: Resume with different values, backtracking on failure.
  \item \textbf{Async/await}: Resume the continuation later, when an I/O operation completes.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Effects and Continuations]
\label{thm:effects-continuations}
Algebraic effect handlers can be implemented using delimited continuations. Conversely, delimited continuations can be encoded as algebraic effects with a single \texttt{shift} operation and a handler that interprets it.
\end{theorem}

\section{Continuation Semantics for Exceptions: Detailed Treatment}
\label{sec:cont-exceptions-detailed}

\subsection{Typed Exceptions}
\label{sec:typed-exceptions}

Modern languages feature \emph{typed} exceptions, where different exception types can be caught by different handlers. The continuation semantics must model a \emph{stack} of handlers, each associated with a type:

\begin{definition}[Exception Handler Stack]
\label{def:handler-stack}
The \textbf{exception handler stack} $H$ is a list of pairs $(\tau_i, \kappa_i)$ where $\tau_i$ is an exception type and $\kappa_i$ is the handler continuation for that type.
\end{definition}

\begin{semrules}{Continuation Semantics: Typed Exceptions}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{try } S_1 \textbf{ catch } \tau\; x \to S_2}\, \rho\, \sigma\, \kappa\, H &= \semS \sembrack{S_1}\, \rho\, \sigma\, \kappa\, ((\tau, \lambda \sigma'.\, \semS \sembrack{S_2}\, \rho[x \mapsto \sigma']\, \sigma'\, \kappa\, H) :: H) \\
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{throw } e}\, \rho\, \sigma\, \kappa\, H &= \text{let } (v, \sigma') = \semE \sembrack{e}\, \rho\, \sigma \text{ in } \text{find\_handler}(v, H, \sigma')
\end{semrules}

\begin{definition}[Handler Search]
\label{def:handler-search}
$\text{find\_handler}(v, H, \sigma)$ searches the handler stack $H$ for the first handler $(\tau, \kappa)$ such that $v : \tau$. If found, it invokes $\kappa\, \sigma$. If no matching handler is found, the exception is uncaught and the program terminates with an error.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Exception Safety and Resource Management}
\label{sec:exception-safety}

The continuation semantics of exceptions reveals a fundamental challenge: \emph{resource management}. When an exception unwinds the continuation stack, any resources (locks, file handles, memory) acquired by the discarded continuation frames must be released.

\begin{definition}[Exception Safety Levels]
\label{def:exception-safety}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Basic guarantee}: No resource leaks on exception.
  \item \textbf{Strong guarantee}: Either the operation succeeds or the program state is rolled back to its pre-operation state.
  \item \textbf{No-throw guarantee}: The operation never throws an exception.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Languages address this through various mechanisms:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{finally blocks} (Java, C\#): Execute cleanup code regardless of whether an exception occurred.
  \item \textbf{RAII} (C++): Destructors are called during stack unwinding.
  \item \textbf{with statements} (Python): Context managers handle resource cleanup.
\end{itemize}

In continuation semantics, \textbf{finally} is modeled by wrapping the continuation with a cleanup action:

\begin{semrules}{Continuation Semantics: Finally}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{try } S_1 \textbf{ finally } S_2}\, \rho\, \sigma\, \kappa &= \semS \sembrack{S_1}\, \rho\, \sigma\, (\lambda \sigma'.\, \semS \sembrack{S_2}\, \rho\, \sigma'\, \kappa) \\
  &\quad \text{with handler } (\lambda \sigma'.\, \semS \sembrack{S_2}\, \rho\, \sigma'\, \text{rethrow})
\end{semrules}

The handler ensures that $S_2$ is executed both on normal completion and on exception.

\section{Defunctionalization of Continuations}
\label{sec:defunctionalization}

\subsection{From Higher-Order to First-Order}
\label{sec:defunc-intro}

Reynolds's \emph{defunctionalization} (1972) transforms a higher-order program into a first-order one by replacing function values with data constructors. When applied to continuations, this yields an abstract machine with an explicit control stack.

\begin{definition}[Defunctionalization]
\label{def:defunctionalization}
Given a program with higher-order functions, \textbf{defunctionalization}:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Enumerates all function values that can arise at runtime
  \item Replaces each function with a data constructor carrying its free variables
  \item Replaces function application with an \texttt{apply} function that pattern-matches on the data constructor
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Defunctionalizing Continuations]
\label{ex:defunc-cont}
Consider the continuation semantics of $e_1 + e_2$:
\[
\semE \sembrack{e_1 + e_2}\, \rho\, \kappa = \semE \sembrack{e_1}\, \rho\, (\lambda v_1.\, \semE \sembrack{e_2}\, \rho\, (\lambda v_2.\, \kappa(v_1 + v_2)))
\]
The continuations that arise are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\lambda v_1.\, \semE \sembrack{e_2}\, \rho\, (\lambda v_2.\, \kappa(v_1 + v_2))$ --- captured $e_2$, $\rho$, $\kappa$
  \item $\lambda v_2.\, \kappa(v_1 + v_2)$ --- captured $v_1$, $\kappa$
\end{itemize}
Defunctionalization replaces these with data types:
\begin{verbatim}
  data Cont = K0                   (* initial continuation *)
            | K1 Expr Env Cont     (* waiting for e1; evaluate e2 next *)
            | K2 Int Cont          (* waiting for e2; add v1 and continue *)
  
  apply :: Cont -> Int -> Ans
  apply K0 v           = answer v
  apply (K1 e2 rho k) v1 = eval e2 rho (K2 v1 k)
  apply (K2 v1 k) v2   = apply k (v1 + v2)
\end{verbatim}
The result is a first-order abstract machine with an explicit stack of continuation frames.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Defunctionalization Correctness]
\label{thm:defunc-correct}
Defunctionalization preserves the semantics of the original program: the defunctionalized program produces the same results as the higher-order original.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{From CPS to Abstract Machines}
\label{sec:cps-to-machine}

The defunctionalization of CPS yields a systematic derivation of abstract machines:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Start with a denotational semantics in continuation-passing style.
  \item Identify all continuation abstractions ($\lambda$-expressions of type $\Cont$).
  \item Defunctionalize: replace each continuation abstraction with a data constructor.
  \item The result is a state-transition system (abstract machine) with an explicit control stack.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}[CEK Machine from CPS]
\label{ex:cek-from-cps}
Defunctionalizing the CPS semantics of the $\lambda$-calculus yields the CEK machine:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Control}: the current expression
  \item \textbf{Environment}: variable bindings
  \item \textbf{Kontinuation}: a stack of frames (Evaluate, Apply, Halt)
\end{itemize}
Each frame corresponds to a defunctionalized continuation constructor.
\end{example}

This derivation technique, due to Danvy (2004) and further developed by others, provides a principled way to derive abstract machines from semantic definitions, ensuring that the machine correctly implements the semantics.
